    {
      "@id": "http://example.org/multimodal-taxonomy#dimension_e0793a1b",
      "@type": "BackgroundKnowledge",
      "instance_name": "islamic_countries_alcohol_ban",
      "label": "Alcohol Ban in Islamic Countries",
      "description": "Islamic countries often ban alcohol consumption based on religious principles, particularly those derived from Sharia law, which prohibits intoxicants. This ban is rooted in religious doctrine, not merely cultural preference, and is enforced by legal systems in many Muslim-majority nations. Understanding this requires knowledge of Islamic jurisprudence and the role of religion in governance in these countries.",
      "belongsToAnalysis": {
        "@id": "http://example.org/multimodal-taxonomy#analysis_21f32587"
      },
      "extractionMethod": "llm_extraction_huggingface"
    },
    {
      "@id": "http://example.org/multimodal-taxonomy#dimension_d95ca7dc",
      "@type": "BackgroundKnowledge",
      "instance_name": "western_countries_alcohol_consumption",
      "label": "Alcohol Consumption in Western Countries",
      "description": "Western countries, particularly those in Europe and North America, generally permit alcohol consumption, often regulating it through taxation, age restrictions, and public health campaigns. This permissive stance is rooted in secular legal systems and cultural norms that do not prohibit alcohol based on religious doctrine. The meme implicitly contrasts this with Islamic countries, requiring knowledge of Western secularism and cultural norms.",
      "belongsToAnalysis": {
        "@id": "http://example.org/multimodal-taxonomy#analysis_21f32587"
      },
      "extractionMethod": "llm_extraction_huggingface"
    },
    {
      "@id": "http://example.org/multimodal-taxonomy#dimension_2e49a356",
      "@type": "BackgroundKnowledge",
      "instance_name": "islamophobia",
      "label": "Islamophobia",
      "description": "The meme implicitly relies on a stereotype that portrays Islamic countries as 'backward' or 'oppressive' for banning alcohol, while suggesting Western countries are more 'liberal' or 'progressive' for allowing it. This framing can be seen as reinforcing Islamophobic tropes that portray Muslims as incompatible with Western values or as inherently oppressive. Understanding the meme requires awareness of this stereotype and its real-world implications.",
      "belongsToAnalysis": {
        "@id": "http://example.org/multimodal-taxonomy#analysis_21f32587"
      },
      "extractionMethod": "llm_extraction_huggingface"
    },
    {
      "@id": "http://example.org/multimodal-taxonomy#dimension_e38e635a",
      "@type": "BackgroundKnowledge",
      "instance_name": "cultural_relativism",
      "label": "Cultural Relativism",
      "description": "The meme implicitly challenges the viewer to consider whether cultural practices should be judged by external standards. It suggests that if alcohol is banned in one culture (Islamic), it should also be banned in another (Western), implying a moral equivalence. This requires understanding of the concept of cultural relativism — the idea that cultural norms should be understood within their own context rather than judged by external standards — which is often used to critique such comparisons.",
      "belongsToAnalysis": {
        "@id": "http://example.org/multimodal-taxonomy#analysis_21f32587"
      },
      "extractionMethod": "llm_extraction_huggingface"
    },
    {
      "@id": "http://example.org/multimodal-taxonomy#dimension_19ffea8f",
      "@type": "BackgroundKnowledge",
      "instance_name": "sharia_law",
      "label": "Sharia Law",
      "description": "Sharia law is a system of religious law derived from the Quran and Hadith, which governs various aspects of life, including moral conduct, family law, and criminal law. The prohibition of alcohol in many Islamic countries is based on Sharia law, which considers alcohol an impure substance. Understanding the meme requires knowledge of Sharia law and its role in Islamic societies.",
      "belongsToAnalysis": {
        "@id": "http://example.org/multimodal-taxonomy#analysis_21f32587"
      },
      "extractionMethod": "llm_extraction_huggingface"
    },
    {
      "@id": "http://example.org/multimodal-taxonomy#dimension_65770582",
      "@type": "BackgroundKnowledge",
      "instance_name": "western_consumerism",
      "label": "Western Consumerism",
      "description": "The juxtaposition of car keys and alcohol in the image may imply a critique of Western consumerism, where material possessions (like cars) are associated with freedom and success, while alcohol is seen as a symbol of indulgence or excess. This requires knowledge of Western cultural values and consumerist ideals, which are often linked to individualism and materialism.",
      "belongsToAnalysis": {
        "@id": "http://example.org/multimodal-taxonomy#analysis_21f32587"
      },
      "extractionMethod": "llm_extraction_huggingface"
    },
    {
      "@id": "http://example.org/multimodal-taxonomy#dimension_49825fb6",
      "@type": "EmotionExpression",
      "instance_name": "contempt",
      "label": "contemptuous emotion",
      "description": "The emotion expressed is contempt, conveyed through the juxtaposition of alcohol and car keys, and the rhetorical question in the text, which implies a dismissive or superior attitude toward Islamic countries' alcohol bans while suggesting Western countries are morally inconsistent. The visual contrast of the car keys (symbolizing materialism or freedom) and alcohol (symbolizing indulgence) reinforces a judgmental tone.",
      "belongsToAnalysis": {
        "@id": "http://example.org/multimodal-taxonomy#analysis_21f32587"
      },
      "extractionMethod": "llm_extraction_huggingface"
    },
    {
